2026-06-14 15:29:32.384 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Data Backups provide limited but meaningful support for recovery from T0816 (Device Restart/Shutdown) attacks. The control's primary value lies in post-incident recovery rather than prevention or rapid eradication. When an adversary forcibly restarts or shuts down a device, the immediate operational disruption occurs regardless of backup availability—the technique's core impact (service interruption and potential negative impact on physical processes during critical states) is not mitigated. However, backups do facilitate recovery by enabling restoration of device configurations and control logic after the restart/shutdown event concludes. In a best-case scenario with well-maintained, tested backups and rapid restoration procedures, this could reduce recovery time by 25-50%: administrators can quickly restore the last known good configuration rather than manually rebuilding complex engineering logic or negotiating with adversaries. The control does not address attacker persistence beyond providing a clean restoration baseline. The 3-2-1-1-0 strategy mentioned enhances reliability of the backup itself but does not accelerate the physical restart/shutdown that has already occurred or its immediate consequences. Recovery speed depends heavily on backup accessibility, device reimaging procedures, and operational complexity—factors not guaranteed by backup existence alone. The control is architecturally relevant to post-incident recovery but offers no prevention of the technique itself and only moderate acceleration of the recovery process in typical OT environments."}
