2026-06-14 16:28:52.859 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Failsafe mechanisms directly address the core threat of T0827 (Loss of Control) by providing automatic, passive safety boundaries that prevent catastrophic system failures even when adversaries have achieved loss of control over normal command channels. These mechanical elements (pressure relief valves, flares, rupture discs, etc.) operate independently of compromised control systems and automatically activate when system parameters exceed safe thresholds, preventing runaway conditions like the uncontrolled blast furnace shutdown described in the technique. This enables rapid functional recovery by containing damage and restoring safe operational parameters without requiring operator intervention or malware eradication—the system reverts to a degraded but safe state automatically. However, the rating does not reach 5 because: (1) failsafe mechanisms are passive protections that prevent catastrophic failure but do not actively restore normal operations or remove attacker persistence; (2) they may require manual intervention to resume full operational capacity after activation; (3) they are often destructive when operated (e.g., venting pressure relief), so recovery still involves some operational disruption; (4) in complex integrated systems, failsafes on individual components may not prevent loss of control across the broader process; and (5) they cannot address techniques where adversaries specifically target or disable the failsafe mechanisms themselves. The control reduces time to recovery from potential total system loss to a managed safe-state shutdown (estimated 75-90% reduction in worst-case recovery time), making it a highly effective but not complete mitigation."}
2026-06-14 16:28:57.773 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0880, technique_name=Loss of Safety, technique_description=Adversaries may compromise safety system functions designed to maintain safe operation of a process when unacceptable or dangerous conditions occur. Safety systems are often composed of the same elements as control systems but have the sole purpose of ensuring the process fails in a predetermined safe manner. Many unsafe conditions in process control happen too quickly for a human operator to react to. Speed is critical in correcting these conditions to limit serious impacts such as Loss of Control and Property Damage. Adversaries may target and disable safety system functions as a prerequisite to subsequent attack execution or to allow for future unsafe conditionals to go unchecked. Detection of a Loss of Safety by operators can result in the shutdown of a process due to strict policies regarding safety systems. This can cause a Loss of Productivity and Revenue and may meet the technical goals of adversaries seeking to cause process disruptions., control_name=Failsafe mechanisms, control_description=Failsafe mechanisms are mechanical elements that prevent physical systems from failing catastrophically even during disastrous events. Like interlocks, they are primarily a safety control that nevertheless serves a secondary purpose in the event of cyber-induced loss of control. Examples include pressure relief valves, flares, rupture discs, and assorted other mechanisms.

