2026-06-14 16:29:03.662 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Failsafe mechanisms provide substantial protection against Loss of Safety attacks by ensuring that if safety system functions are compromised or disabled, the physical process will automatically transition to a safe state without requiring operator intervention or system recovery. These mechanical devices (pressure relief valves, flares, rupture discs, etc.) operate independently of cyber-controlled systems and activate based on physical parameters, making them resistant to cyberattack vectors targeting software-based safety logic.\n\nStrengths: (1) Failsafe mechanisms enable rapid recovery to safe operational states—activation is often instantaneous upon detecting hazardous conditions, greatly reducing time to restoration compared to detecting compromise, investigating root cause, and manually recovering safety functions; (2) They provide defense-in-depth by mitigating the impact even if safety control system compromise succeeds, preventing cascading failures into dangerous physical states; (3) They remove attacker persistence in terms of operational impact—even if an adversary disables cyber safety functions, the mechanical failsafe prevents the unsafe condition from propagating.\n\nLimitations: (1) Failsafe mechanisms do not directly eradicate the cyber compromise itself—the attacker's presence in safety system code/configuration remains until formal forensics and system remediation occur; (2) Recovery of full safety system functionality still requires identifying and removing the attacker's modifications, which failsafes do not accelerate; (3) Some failsafe designs may be destructive (e.g., flares venting product), creating operational disruption even during successful containment; (4) Failsafes are often difficult to retrofit or modify, limiting their deployment flexibility.\n\nThe 4-rating reflects that failsafe mechanisms reduce recovery time by 75-90% for the critical safety restoration phase by ensuring process safety is maintained mechanically while cyber recovery proceeds in parallel, though complete eradication of the attacker's presence still requires separate remediation efforts."}
