2026-06-14 16:29:18.557 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Failsafe mechanisms provide strong mitigation for Loss of Protection attacks by serving as automated, hardware-based safety controls that operate independently of compromised software or protective system functions. When adversaries disable or compromise protective system functions (such as alarm systems, shutdown controllers, or safety interlocks), failsafe mechanisms like pressure relief valves, rupture discs, and flares continue to function passively, preventing catastrophic equipment failure and hazardous conditions. This significantly reduces both the impact of the attack and the complexity of recovery. By automatically containing faults and abnormal conditions that would otherwise cascade, failsafe mechanisms enable faster restoration of normal operations since operators are not forced into emergency shutdowns triggered by uncontrolled process deviations. The hardware-based nature of these mechanisms makes them immune to the cyber-induced loss of protection that affects software-based protective functions. However, the control does not achieve a perfect 5 rating because: (1) failsafe mechanisms are often inherently destructive when activated (e.g., venting, rupturing), which may complicate recovery procedures and require equipment replacement; (2) they do not directly facilitate detection and removal of the attacker's compromise from the protective system itself; (3) they require proper mechanical maintenance and design to remain effective, and retrofitting may not be feasible in all systems; and (4) in some industrial processes, even failsafe activation may not prevent all process disruptions or hazards. Nevertheless, they reduce recovery time by 75-90% compared to scenarios without failsafe protections by preventing uncontrolled escalation and enabling controlled shutdown rather than catastrophic failure."}
